WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.471
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.471 --> 00:00:13.600
Hi.
Today we will talk about the arms race

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.730
between the researchers trying
to develop an HIV vaccine, and

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.340
the rapidly evolving human
immunodeficiency virus.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:27.360
And this discussion will bring us to
the challenge of comparing highly diverse

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.290
viruses from different patients, or
comparing highly diverse proteins

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.005
from species separated by
millions of years of evolution.

00:00:36.005 --> 00:00:40.200
We will learn that the traditional
sickness alignment algorithm

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.190
that we studied before
often fails to detect

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:48.490
subtle similarity between
highly diverged sequences.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:53.400
To address this limitation, we will
develop a new computational framework

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.340
called Hidden Markov Models,
whose applications in

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:02.480
bioinformatics will extend well
beyond sequence comparison.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.670
We will start from classifying
HIV phenotypes, and

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:12.920
asking the question of what HIV
phenotype Gaëtan Dugas had.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.990
Gaëtan Dugas was a flight attendant
who traveled the world and

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:21.250
engaged in sexual liaison
with hundreds of men.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:27.030
In 1984, he has become the most
studied HIV patient in the world,

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:32.300
when biologists constructed
a network of 40 HIV patients

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.650
whom he infected with his HIV virus.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:40.330
He died in the same year and
was named AIDS patient zero,

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:44.430
although, of course, he was not
the first HIV patient.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.270
In the same year, the U.S. Health Secretary,

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:53.570
Margaret Heckler, announced that an HIV
vaccine is about to be developed.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:59.060
13 years later, Bill Clinton said:
"It's no longer a question of

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:04.531
whether we can develop an AIDS vaccine;
it is simply a question of when."

00:02:04.531 --> 00:02:09.290
Well, we still don't have
an HIV vaccine today,

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:11.860
and to understand why we don't have it,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:17.680
it's important to figure out how
HIV evades the human immune systems.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:23.050
Vaccines are often made from
virus surface proteins to train

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:28.800
the human immune system to recognize
vital envelope proteins and destroy them.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:35.050
However, the strategy didn't work for
HIV viruses because HIV evolves so

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:42.210
fast that there is actually
a multitude of various HIV subtypes.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:48.990
This slide shows ten sequences from
HIV envelope glycoprotein gp120

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.880
that shows a large number of substitutions,
insertions, and

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.580
deletions within a single patient.

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:03.060
HIV viruses in a single patient
evolve with a very

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:08.910
fast rate of 2% per nucleotide per year.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.650
HIV strains in different patients are so

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:14.810
diverged that they require
different drug cocktails.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:20.370
Today we will talk about one specific

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.060
HIV phenotype called
syncytium-inducing phenotype.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.500
HIV has the ability to trick human cells

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.400
to fuse together together
to form one giant cell.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.800
Why would the HIV virus do
such a strange thing?

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:42.160
Because it's easier to kill all the cells
once they are fused in a single cell.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.650
The question we will be interested in today:
"Given an HIV sequence,

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:53.270
can we figure out whether it has
a syncytium-inducing phenotype or not?"

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:58.740
And in this slide,
you see 20 sequences from protein gp120

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.520
actually, from a spectacularly conserved
region of this protein called the V3 loop.

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:11.110
Despite the fact that it's conserved,
the sequences have many mutations

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:15.370
and even have different lengths, so
we would definitely need to align them.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:20.970
We know that six of these sequences
have syncytium-inducing phenotype,

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.573
but what sequence features

00:04:22.573 --> 00:04:27.830
define this syncytium-inducing phenotype?

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:34.660
Biologists noticed that in HIV viruses
with the syncytium-inducing phenotype,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:39.390
the amino acids at positions 11 and 25

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:44.690
of the V3 region are Arginine and
Leucine, and it has become

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:50.280
the first computational rule for
classifying the syncytium-inducing phenotype.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.850
It later turned out that the classifying
rules are much more complex, but

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.715
we will not go into the details
of the more complex rules.

00:04:56.715 --> 00:05:02.332
What we will, however, notice
is that when we construct this alignment

00:05:02.332 --> 00:05:06.648
and build a profile and
sequence logo from this alignment,

00:05:06.648 --> 00:05:11.264
we will see the conservation
of sequences is extremely

00:05:11.264 --> 00:05:15.420
non-uniform across different positions.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.840
For example, positions 11 and 25
are particularly poorly in this case.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.920
And this brings one challenge and

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:31.060
one question: the challenge is
to predict an HIV phenotype.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.390
It is very important to
align sequences correctly.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:41.400
In our case, alignment of the
V3 region is not difficult.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:44.510
We can figure out that
all V3 regions

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:47.390
belong to the same type of proteins.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:52.950
However, aligning them correctly
so that all amino acids

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:58.400
belonging to columns 11 and 25 appear in
the same column may become a challenge.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.780
And it brings the following question:

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:06.140
Was it a good idea to use
the same scoring matrix

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:08.320
across different columns of an alignment,

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.550
something we have done in our previous
studies of multiple alignment?

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:19.117
And a brief look at this sequence logo
tells us that we should develop a new

00:06:19.117 --> 00:06:23.403
statistically solid problem
formulation for alignment

00:06:23.403 --> 00:06:28.393
that will use a different scoring
approach at different columns.

